<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/foundational/what-makes-a-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    What makes a rule a <em>law</em> rather than a command, a custom or a threat? The classic answer:
    a law is a rule made by a recognised authority, applying to everyone in its scope, backed by the
    state's enforcement — and, crucially, made and changed by <em>procedures</em> that are themselves
    law. That last clause is the deep one. In a legal system, the rule-maker's power has limits set by
    further rules: a parliament that legislates by process, courts that apply rules by method,
    officials whose powers come from enabling acts. "Law" is therefore not one thing but a
    <strong>stack of sources</strong>, each trumping the one below: a <strong>constitution</strong> sets
    the frame; <strong>statutes</strong> are what legislatures write; <strong>delegated
    legislation</strong> is the executive filling in detail; <strong>precedent</strong> is what courts
    said it all means; and custom quietly fills the gaps. A lawyer's first skill is knowing which
    source controls a question — and what to do when two seem to conflict. This lesson walks the whole
    system as England's does it (the syllabus's model system): where law comes from, how judges read
    it, the courts it runs through, the people who staff them, and the ideas — morality, justice, the
    rule of law — that surround the machine and drive its repair.
  </p>
  <p>
    Law is also inseparable from <strong>society</strong>: it changes as the society it governs changes.
    Statutes on divorce, privacy and data protection are found nowhere in medieval law because the
    problems are new; and conversely, law acts back on society — the wearing of seatbelts became
    near-universal within a decade of being required, because legal rules both express norms and teach
    them. The relationship runs both directions, and studying it means watching which way the causation
    flows in each case.
  </p>

  <h2>Law, morality, justice — and the rule of law</h2>
  <p>
    Three ideals stand next to law, sometimes inside it, sometimes over it. <strong>Law and
    morality</strong>: most law encodes moral judgements (murder is both), but not all morality is law
    (lying to a friend) and — the sharpest point — not all law is moral, as every unjust statute in
    history shows. The legal-philosophy question "is an unjust law still law?" divides
    <strong>positivists</strong> (law is what the recognised process produced; validity and merit are
    separate — Hart) from <strong>natural-law theorists</strong> (genuinely lawless rules fail as law —
    Fuller, whose "eight ways to fail to make law" describe a legal system's internal morality). The
    related <strong>law and justice</strong> question is different: justice asks whether outcomes are
    <em>fair</em> — a law can be valid, obeyed, and unjust, which is exactly why reform machinery
    exists. The structural answer to both is the <strong>rule of law</strong>: the principle that
    government itself operates under law. A. V. Dicey's classic three limbs — no one may be punished
    except for a breach of law established in the ordinary courts (<em>no arbitrary power</em>);
    everyone, however powerful, is subject to the same law (<em>equality before the law</em>); and
    rights are secured by court decisions, not just paper declarations. Modern formulations (the World
    Justice Project's index) add accessible courts, independent judges and open government. The rule of
    law is law's own conscience — and, practically, the precondition for the predictability that makes
    contracts, investment and daily life possible.
  </p>

  <h2>How law is made: Parliament</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Parliamentary law making</strong> is the system's engine. The process is deliberately
    slow: a <em>green paper</em> floats policy options for consultation, a <em>white paper</em> states
    the government's chosen policy, then a <em>bill</em> passes through stages in both Houses of
    Parliament — first reading (introduced), second reading (debate on principle), committee stage
    (line-by-line scrutiny, amendments), report and third reading — before <em>Royal Assent</em>, the
    constitutional formality that makes it an Act. The friction is the feature: an idea must survive
    public consultation, two chambers and detailed committee scrutiny before touching anyone's rights.
    Parliament also delegates: <strong>delegated legislation</strong> is law made by others under powers
    an Act grants — ministers issue <em>statutory instruments</em> (the technical detail of, say,
    housing benefit rules; thousands per year, far outnumbering Acts), local authorities pass
    <em>by-laws</em>, and the Privy Council issues <em>orders in council</em>. Delegation trades
    democratic scrutiny for speed and expertise — which is why the enabling Act is meant to confine it,
    why a committee (the Joint Committee on Statutory Instruments) checks it, and why courts can strike
    down delegated legislation that goes beyond its parent Act (<em>ultra vires</em>). When statutes
    themselves need renewal, the <strong>Law Commission</strong> (a standing body since 1965) reviews
    whole areas of law, consults, and proposes consolidated reform — the machinery of planned
    <strong>law reform</strong> rather than piecemeal patching. And for five decades European law sat
    over all of it: as an EU member, the UK treated directly applicable EU regulations and directives
    as part of domestic law, and courts disapplied inconsistent statutes; since Brexit (withdrawal
    took effect 31 January 2020) that layer became <em>retained EU law</em> — kept unless repealed or
    reformed — but the lesson remains: a legal system's stack can have layers above parliament, and
    sovereignty arguments follow the layering.
  </p>

  <h2>How law is read: statutory interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    Statutes are written in language, and language under-determines cases — so judges need methods.
    The <strong>literal rule</strong> reads the words plainly even if the result is absurd (the famous
    case law turns on whether "in the vicinity of" a building meant the pavement or the interior);
    the <strong>golden rule</strong> departs from the plain meaning to avoid absurdity; the
    <strong>mischief rule</strong> asks what defect the statute was passed to cure and reads it to cure
    that defect; the modern <strong>purposive approach</strong> — now strengthened in the UK by the
    Human Rights Act 1998 (which requires statutes to be read compatibly with Convention rights where
    possible) — asks what purpose the statute serves. The rules matter because the same sentence can
    license any of the four readings, and which one wins decides real cases. Alongside the approaches
    run <em>presumptions</em> and internal aids (long title, schedules) — and a whole craft of reading
    definitions ("includes" vs "means"), exceptions and cross-references. Interpretation is where
    law's claim to be a reasoning discipline is tested daily: not what the judge prefers, but what the
    text, its purpose and the system's methods will bear.
  </p>

  <h2>How law grows: judicial precedent</h2>
  <p>
    The other great law-making source is the courts themselves, and it works like this:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="precedent — how one case quietly becomes the law for the next thousand" widget="equationStep" config={precedentCfg} />

  <p>
    Precedent is the common law system's compounding technology: a decision on a new problem (a novel
    technology, a new tort) becomes the rule for the next thousand like cases, and law accretes
    case-by-case over centuries. It has its own grammar: the binding part is the <em>ratio
    decidendi</em>, while everything else a judge says (<em>obiter dicta</em> — "by the way") only
    persuades. The hierarchy of courts fixes who binds whom: the Supreme Court binds all below it,
    the Court of Appeal binds itself save in narrow exceptions, and every court binds the ones below.
    <strong>Distinguishing</strong> is the safety valve — a later court shows the facts genuinely
    differ, so the rule does not reach the case — and it is why the common law can evolve without
    revolution: the doctrine demands consistency, and the craft supplies the exceptions. Precedent's
    virtues are predictability and equality (like cases alike); its vice is inertia, which is exactly
    what overruling and the reform machinery exist to check.
  </p>

  <h2>The legal system: courts, people, and access</h2>
  <p>
    The machine has two tracks. <strong>Civil courts</strong> settle disputes between parties —
    contract, tort, family money — starting (in England) mostly at the County Court or High Court,
    with appeal upward to the Court of Appeal and Supreme Court. The civil track also includes
    alternatives: tribunals for specialist disputes (employment, immigration), and
    <strong>alternative dispute resolution</strong> — mediation (a neutral helps the parties settle),
    arbitration (a private adjudicator's decision the parties agreed in advance to accept), and
    negotiation — which resolves the large majority of disputes before any judge hears them, because
    trials are slow, public and expensive. <strong>Criminal courts</strong> prosecute offences against
    the state: summary cases in the Magistrates' Court, either-way and indictable cases through the
    Crown Court, with appeals upward. Lay people are built into this track deliberately:
    <strong>magistrates</strong> — unpaid, trained volunteers — decide the vast majority of criminal
    cases, and the <strong>jury</strong> of 12 randomly selected citizens decides guilt in serious
    trials, the system's oldest statement that conviction should require ordinary people's consent.
    <strong>Legal personnel</strong> run the machinery: solicitors advise and prepare, barristers
    specialise in advocacy and court argument, and the <strong>judiciary</strong> — appointed on merit,
    protected by security of tenure and salary, trained increasingly formally — is the branch whose
    independence the rule of law depends on; a judge who can be dismissed for an unpopular ruling is a
    rule-of-law failure by definition. Finally, <strong>access to justice</strong>: rights without the
    means to enforce them are decoration. The machinery includes legal aid (public funding of legal
    representation for those who qualify — sharply narrowed by the Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment
    of Offenders Act 2012, with consequences still debated), duty solicitors, the small-claims track
    that lets people sue without lawyers, and charities like Citizens Advice. Funding is the system's
    most persistent fault line: a perfect procedure with no affordable entry is, for most people, a
    locked door.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the criminal and civil systems are not two names for one thing.
  They differ in <em>parties</em> (the state prosecutes; private parties sue), <em>purpose</em>
  (punishment vs compensation), <em>outcomes</em> (prison and fines vs damages and orders) and
  <em>standard of proof</em> — beyond reasonable doubt in criminal trials, the balance of probabilities
  in civil cases. O. J. Simpson was acquitted criminally but found liable civilly for the same events:
  not a contradiction, but two different questions asked under two different standards.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three debates run underneath everything above. <strong>Separation of powers</strong>: parliament
    makes law, the executive applies it, courts adjudicate it — and the UK's "constitution" enforces
    the separation not by a single written document but by statutes (the Human Rights Act 1998
    requiring ministers to declare compatibility with rights; the Constitutional Reform Act 2005,
    which created the Supreme Court and tightened judicial appointments) and convention. Courts can
    declare an Act incompatible with Convention rights — but cannot strike it down; parliament remains
    sovereign, and the pressure that declaration generates is the intended remedy.
    <strong>Hart v Devlin</strong>: when may the criminal law enforce morality? Lord Devlin (after the
    1957 Wolfenden report on homosexuality and prostitution) argued society's shared morality deserves
    protection like its physical order; H. L. A. Hart argued harm to others, not moral disgust, is the
    proper trigger — the debate behind every subsequent argument about what the criminal law should
    reach. And <strong>the question the whole system exists for</strong>: every rule you will read in
    this subject's ladder — precedent, interpretation rules, appeal routes, juries, legal aid — exists
    because someone, somewhere, was harmed in a way the previous rules couldn't answer. Law is a moral
    technology under permanent repair, and its mechanics are worth knowing precisely so that the
    repair can be argued for in the system's own language: amendment, appeal, precedent, reform.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Dicey, Introduction to the Study of the Law of the Constitution (1885); Hart, The Concept of Law (1961); Fuller, The Morality of Law (1964); Devlin, The Enforcement of Morals (1965); Glanville Williams, Learning the Law; Law Commission Act 1965; Constitutional Reform Act 2005.</p>
</TopicPage>
